 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 526535,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A crafter uses four letter-size sheets of paper to create a paper mosaic in the shape of a square. They decide they want to frame the paper mosaic, so they measure and determine that the area of the paper mosaic is <span data-math=\"\\text{2,412.89 cm}^2\">\\text{2,412.89 cm}^2</span>. Does their calculation make sense? Explain your answer.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>A typical sheet of paper is 8.5 inches by 11 inches. They cut up four sheets of this paper to make their mosaic. Eleven inches is a little bit less than the length of a ruler, which is around 30 cm. So, you know the upper limit in 30 by 30 times 4, which is way overestimating since 8 is far less than 11.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"30(30)(4) = 3600\\ {\\rm cm}^{2}\"></span></p>\n<p>Since their calculation is 2,412.80 square centimeters, their calculation does make sense.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Yes. The unit of value, cm<sup>2</sup>, is appropriate given the size of a sheet of paper. Because the total area of 2,412.89 cm<sup>2</sup> represents four sheets of paper, each sheet is approximately 600 cm<sup>2</sup>, which seems reasonable for a sheet of letter-size paper."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
